  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"116\" data-end=\"302\"><strong data-start=\"116\" data-end=\"129\">Factor XI</strong> is a clotting factor involved in the <strong data-start=\"167\" data-end=\"188\">intrinsic pathway</strong> of the coagulation cascade. It plays a critical role in sustaining thrombin generation and stable clot formation.</p>\n<p data-start=\"304\" data-end=\"347\">The <strong data-start=\"308\" data-end=\"335\">Factor XI Activity test</strong> is used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"349\" data-end=\"610\">\n<li data-start=\"349\" data-end=\"415\">\n<p data-start=\"351\" data-end=\"415\">Diagnose <strong data-start=\"360\" data-end=\"384\">Factor XI deficiency</strong> (also called <strong data-start=\"398\" data-end=\"414\">Hemophilia C</strong>)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"416\" data-end=\"485\">\n<p data-start=\"418\" data-end=\"485\">Evaluate <strong data-start=\"427\" data-end=\"485\">prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT)</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"486\" data-end=\"531\">\n<p data-start=\"488\" data-end=\"531\">Investigate unexplained bleeding tendencies</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"532\" data-end=\"610\">\n<p data-start=\"534\" data-end=\"610\">Assess bleeding risk before surgery in individuals with low Factor XI levels</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"612\" data-end=\"753\">Factor XI deficiency is rare and inherited in an <strong data-start=\"661\" data-end=\"684\">autosomal recessive</strong> pattern, more common in individuals of <strong data-start=\"724\" data-end=\"752\">Ashkenazi Jewish descent</strong>.</p>\n<p data-start=\"612\" data-end=\"753\">Elevated Factor XI activity levels are not typically a concern in routine practice but may be associated with:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Explanation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Thrombotic disorders (possible risk factor)</strong></td>\n<td>Some studies suggest high Factor XI levels may increase clot risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Acute phase response</strong></td>\n<td>May be mildly elevated in systemic inflammation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Estrogen exposure / Pregnancy</strong></td>\n<td>Hormonal changes may cause transient increases</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"612\" data-end=\"753\">&nbsp;</p>",
